cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use the Swarmbly Local Cognitive Extension design in academic work,
  please cite the whitepaper (see `preferred-citation`). It is a draft and has
  not been peer reviewed.
type: software

title: "Swarmbly LCE: Local Cognitive Extension"
abstract: >-
  A local-first extension to the Swarmbly decentralized inference protocol.
  Each user keeps on their own device a human-readable memory (a wiki whose
  claims are anchored to source spans, typed and versioned) and a lightweight
  adapter trained only on behavioural patterns (voice, register, terminology,
  procedures), regenerated from the base model and the memory with
  interleaved replay and gated on freshly generated tests. Personalization
  enters the protocol only through fields of the existing global contract;
  workers serving third parties use their base model, leaving verification
  intact. Nodes may optionally exchange small signed knowledge capsules on
  demand; descendants require new human evidence, prevalence is a label rather
  than an adoption criterion, and preserved capsules use a replica count
  derived from a churn tolerance. The design is derived from evidence on
  knowledge injection, model collapse, conformity and correlated errors across
  model families, and from population-genetic and cultural-evolution
  instruments. Every mechanism carries a pre-registered hypothesis and
  abandonment condition. Since whitepaper v0.1, pre-registered runs with real
  local models have measured lexicon adherence, cross-user homogeneity and
  cross-family error agreement under task projection, the value of local
  memory, and the anchored wiki against raw retrieval (confirmatory: the
  wiki loses on accuracy and is retained as a governance layer), together
  with source forgetting and reproducibility. The repository ships a reference implementation, a
  validation harness whose ten instrument tests pass in simulation, the raw
  outputs of every run and a figure-anchoring test suite; mock and
  simulation outputs are not evidence.

